    subject: 'Physics',
    icon: '⚛️',
    description: 'Kinematics, Newton’s laws, work-energy-power, momentum.',
    cards: [
      { id: 'phy-mot-01', front: 'Speed vs velocity?', back: 'Speed: distance ÷ time (scalar). Velocity: displacement ÷ time (vector).' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-02', front: 'Value and units of g near Earth’s surface?', back: '≈ 9.8 m/s² (acceleration due to gravity).' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-03', front: 'State Newton’s second law in symbols.', back: 'F = ma — force equals mass times acceleration.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-04', front: 'Newton’s first law is the law of…', back: 'Inertia: no net force → no change in motion.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-05', front: 'Newton’s third law pair example?', back: 'Rocket pushes gas down; gas pushes rocket up — equal and opposite.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-06', front: 'Momentum formula, units, and conservation condition?', back: 'p = mv (kg·m/s); conserved when no external force acts.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-07', front: 'Work done — formula and units?', back: 'W = F·d·cosθ, joules (J).' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-08', front: 'Kinetic and gravitational potential energy formulas?', back: 'KE = ½mv²; GPE = mgh.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-09', front: 'Power — formula and units?', back: 'P = work ÷ time, watts (W).' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-10', front: 'State the law of conservation of energy.', back: 'Energy cannot be created or destroyed — only transformed from one form to another.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-11', front: 'Which force always opposes motion between surfaces?', back: 'Friction.' },
      { id: 'phy-mot-12', front: 'A ball thrown up momentarily has acceleration equal to…', back: 'g downward (≈ 9.8 m/s²) — even at the top where v = 0.' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-13', front: 'The scalar (dot) product of two perpendicular vectors is…', back: 'Zero (cos 90° = 0).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-14', front: 'A body at rest on a floor experiences which normal reaction?', back: 'Equal and opposite to its weight (mg) — from Newton’s third law.' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-15', front: 'Impulse equals change in…', back: 'Momentum (J = Δp = FΔt).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-16', front: 'For a projectile, the horizontal velocity is…', back: 'Constant (ignoring air resistance); only the vertical component changes.' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-17', front: 'Tangential velocity in circular motion relates to angular velocity by…', back: 'v = ωr (linear speed = angular speed × radius).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-18', front: 'Centripetal force is directed…', back: 'Toward the centre of the circle (F = mv²/r).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-19', front: 'When the line of action of a force passes through the axis of rotation, the torque is…', back: 'Zero.' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-20', front: 'Work done when force is perpendicular to displacement?', back: 'Zero (W = Fd cos 90° = 0).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-21', front: 'Statement of the work-energy theorem?', back: 'The net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy.' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-22', front: 'A perfectly inelastic collision is one in which…', back: 'The objects stick together and kinetic energy is not conserved (momentum is).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-23', front: 'The centre of mass of a uniform object is at its…', back: 'Geometric centre (e.g. the centre of a uniform rod or sphere).' },
            { id: 'phy-mot-24', front: 'Pressure at a depth h in a fluid is given by…', back: 'P = P₀ + ρgh (atmospheric pressure plus the hydrostatic term).' },
          ],
